window.CIVIL_SET_DATA["past-r2083"] = {"title":"2083 Exam Recall","negativeMarking":0,"chapters":[{"name":"Soil Mechanics and Foundation Engineering","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-017","src":"PAST-R2083-017","text":"The minimum size of silt particles, below which particles are classed as clay, is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"0.002 mm"},{"key":"b","text":"0.02 mm"},{"key":"c","text":"0.075 mm"},{"key":"d","text":"0.0002 mm"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Silt ranges from 0.002 mm (2 µm) to 0.075 mm in the IS classification (0.06 mm in the MIT and BS systems). Finer particles are clay and coarser ones are sand.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":17,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q17 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0201"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-025","src":"PAST-R2083-025","text":"The coefficient of earth pressure at rest (K<sub>0</sub>) is used for a retaining wall that","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Moves away from the backfill"},{"key":"b","text":"Is pushed into the backfill"},{"key":"c","text":"Is rigid and does not yield, such as a basement wall or a wall propped at the top"},{"key":"d","text":"Is free to rotate about its base"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Earth pressure at rest acts when the wall does not move at all, as with basement walls, box culverts and bridge abutments held at the top. K<sub>0</sub> ≈ 1 − sin φ′ (Jaky) lies between K<sub>a</sub> and K<sub>p</sub>. A wall that moves or rotates away from the soil develops active pressure (K<sub>a</sub>), and a wall pushed into the soil develops passive pressure (K<sub>p</sub>).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":25,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q25 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0204"}]},{"name":"Basic Water Resources Engineering","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-001","src":"PAST-R2083-001","text":"A simple U-tube manometer is used for measuring","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Atmospheric pressure only"},{"key":"b","text":"The velocity of flow directly"},{"key":"c","text":"The gauge pressure at a point, including high and negative (vacuum) pressures"},{"key":"d","text":"The discharge in a pipe directly"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"A U-tube manometer balances the pressure in the pipe against a column of heavy liquid such as mercury, so it can read high pressures and negative (vacuum) gauge pressures that a simple piezometer cannot. A differential U-tube measures the pressure difference between two points. Atmospheric pressure is read with a barometer; velocity and discharge need a Pitot tube, venturimeter or orifice meter, which may use a manometer only to read the head difference.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":1,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q1 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0302"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-010","src":"PAST-R2083-010","text":"Which of the following is NOT an assumption of Bernoulli's equation?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The flow is steady"},{"key":"b","text":"The fluid is incompressible"},{"key":"c","text":"The flow is frictionless (ideal fluid)"},{"key":"d","text":"The velocity is the same at every section (uniform flow)"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Bernoulli's equation, p/γ + V<sup>2</sup>/2g + z = constant, assumes steady, incompressible, frictionless flow along a streamline (and irrotational flow if it is applied across streamlines). The velocity may change from section to section, as it does in a venturimeter, so uniform flow is not required.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":10,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q10 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0303"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-020","src":"PAST-R2083-020","text":"For laminar flow in a pipe, the coefficient of friction f′ used in h<sub>f</sub> = 4f′LV<sup>2</sup>/(2gD) is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"64/Re"},{"key":"b","text":"16/Re"},{"key":"c","text":"0.079/Re<sup>0.25</sup>"},{"key":"d","text":"24/Re"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"For laminar flow the Hagen–Poiseuille equation gives h<sub>f</sub> = 32μVL/(γD<sup>2</sup>). Written as h<sub>f</sub> = fLV<sup>2</sup>/(2gD), this makes the Darcy friction factor f = 64/Re, so the coefficient of friction f′ = f/4 = 16/Re. 0.079/Re<sup>0.25</sup> (Blasius) applies to turbulent flow in smooth pipes.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":20,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q20 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0304"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-023","src":"PAST-R2083-023","text":"When pipes of the same diameter are connected in series","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The head loss is the same in each pipe and the discharges add up"},{"key":"b","text":"The discharge and velocity are the same in each pipe, and the total head loss is the sum of the individual losses"},{"key":"c","text":"The velocity changes from one pipe to the next"},{"key":"d","text":"The total head loss equals the loss in the longest pipe"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"In series, the same discharge flows through every pipe (continuity); with equal diameters the velocity is also the same, so the line behaves like one pipe of the combined length. The total head loss is the sum of the losses in the individual pipes plus any minor losses. Equal head loss with the discharges adding up is the rule for pipes in parallel.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":23,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q23 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0304"}]},{"name":"Structural Mechanics","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-002","src":"PAST-R2083-002","text":"If the bending moment is constant along a length of a beam, the shear force over that length is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Zero"},{"key":"b","text":"Maximum"},{"key":"c","text":"Constant but not zero"},{"key":"d","text":"Varying linearly"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Shear force is the rate of change of bending moment, V = dM/dx. A constant M gives dM/dx = 0, so the shear force is zero. This is pure bending, as in the middle part of a simply supported beam carrying two equal loads placed symmetrically.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":2,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q2 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0401"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-003","src":"PAST-R2083-003","text":"The maximum deflection of a cantilever of span L carrying a uniformly distributed load w per unit length over its entire span is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"wL<sup>4</sup>/(3EI)"},{"key":"b","text":"5wL<sup>4</sup>/(384EI)"},{"key":"c","text":"wL<sup>4</sup>/(8EI)"},{"key":"d","text":"wL<sup>4</sup>/(48EI)"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"The deflection is maximum at the free end: wL<sup>4</sup>/(8EI), with a free-end slope of wL<sup>3</sup>/(6EI). 5wL<sup>4</sup>/(384EI) is the central deflection of a simply supported beam under UDL, and WL<sup>3</sup>/(3EI) is for a cantilever with a point load W at the free end.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":3,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q3 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0404"}]},{"name":"Design of Structures","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-005","src":"PAST-R2083-005","text":"As per IS 456, the minimum number of longitudinal bars in a reinforced concrete column is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"3 in rectangular and 4 in circular columns"},{"key":"b","text":"6 in rectangular and 8 in circular columns"},{"key":"c","text":"4 in rectangular and 6 in circular columns"},{"key":"d","text":"4 in both rectangular and circular columns"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"IS 456 (clause 26.5.3.1) requires at least 4 longitudinal bars in a rectangular column and 6 in a circular column, each at least 12 mm in diameter. The total longitudinal steel must be between 0.8% and 6% of the gross area (usually not more than 4% where bars are lapped).","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":5,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q5 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0503"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-016","src":"PAST-R2083-016","text":"The increase in deformation of concrete with time under a constant sustained load is called","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Shrinkage"},{"key":"b","text":"Creep"},{"key":"c","text":"Bleeding"},{"key":"d","text":"Fatigue"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"Creep is the time-dependent strain of concrete under a sustained, constant stress. Shrinkage is a change in volume that occurs without any load, bleeding is water rising to the surface of fresh concrete, and fatigue is failure under repeated loading.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":16,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q16 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0502"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-022","src":"PAST-R2083-022","text":"NBC 202 of the Nepal National Building Code deals with","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Seismic design of buildings in Nepal"},{"key":"b","text":"Mandatory rules of thumb for load-bearing masonry"},{"key":"c","text":"Reinforced concrete buildings without masonry infill"},{"key":"d","text":"Wind loads"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"NBC 202 gives mandatory rules of thumb (ready-to-use requirements) for load-bearing masonry buildings. Seismic design is covered by NBC 105, RC buildings without masonry infill by NBC 205 and wind loads by NBC 104.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":22,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q22 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0506"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-024","src":"PAST-R2083-024","text":"The slump of concrete in the slump test is measured in","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Millimetres"},{"key":"b","text":"N/mm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"c","text":"Seconds"},{"key":"d","text":"Degrees"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Slump is the drop in height of the concrete after the 300 mm high cone is lifted, measured in mm (for example 25–75 mm for ordinary work). Vee-Bee consistency is measured in seconds, the compacting factor has no unit, and N/mm<sup>2</sup> is a unit of strength.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":24,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q24 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0502"}]},{"name":"Water Supply, Sanitation and Environment","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-026","src":"PAST-R2083-026","text":"Which pipe is strong against internal pressure but weak against external loads?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Cast iron pipe"},{"key":"b","text":"Reinforced concrete pipe"},{"key":"c","text":"Asbestos cement pipe"},{"key":"d","text":"Steel pipe"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Steel has high tensile strength, so steel pipes resist high internal pressure, but their thin walls can buckle or collapse under external loads or a vacuum. Cast iron and concrete pipes are the reverse: strong against external loads but less able to carry high internal pressure.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":26,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q26 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0602"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-027","src":"PAST-R2083-027","text":"A drop manhole, in which a vertical pipe connects an incoming sewer to a lower outgoing sewer, is provided when the difference in their invert levels exceeds about","options":[{"key":"a","text":"0.15 m"},{"key":"b","text":"0.3 m"},{"key":"c","text":"0.45 m"},{"key":"d","text":"0.6 m"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"When a branch sewer enters more than about 0.6 m (600 mm) above the outgoing sewer, a vertical drop pipe carries the flow down. This stops sewage falling freely onto the manhole floor, which would splash and erode the benching, and keeps the manhole safe to enter.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":27,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q27 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0604"}]},{"name":"Irrigation and Drainage","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-004","src":"PAST-R2083-004","text":"A cross regulator in a canal is mainly used to","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Pass surplus canal water into a natural drain"},{"key":"b","text":"Head up the water in the parent canal so that the off-taking channels get their full supply when the parent canal runs low"},{"key":"c","text":"Measure the discharge entering a distributary"},{"key":"d","text":"Carry the canal across a natural drain"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"A cross regulator is built across the parent canal just downstream of an off-take. Partly closing its gates raises the upstream water level so the off-takes can draw their supply during low flow; it also allows the reach below to be closed for repairs. A canal escape disposes of surplus water, a head regulator or meter flume controls and measures the discharge of an off-take, and an aqueduct carries a canal over a drain.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":4,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q4 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0705"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-006","src":"PAST-R2083-006","text":"What is the main effect when scouring occurs in an irrigation canal?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Silt deposits on the bed and the canal loses carrying capacity"},{"key":"b","text":"Seepage losses stop because the bed becomes impervious"},{"key":"c","text":"The water level rises and the canal overtops its banks"},{"key":"d","text":"The bed and banks erode, the bed level drops and the water level at the off-takes falls, reducing the command"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Scouring occurs when the velocity exceeds the non-scouring (critical) velocity, so the flow picks up bed and bank material. The bed is lowered and the water surface falls with it, so outlets and off-takes draw less water and the command area shrinks; scour can also undermine canal structures. Silt deposition, loss of capacity and overtopping belong to the opposite problem, silting, which happens when the velocity is too low.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":6,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q6 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0702"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-007","src":"PAST-R2083-007","text":"A syphon aqueduct is provided when","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The canal bed is well above the high flood level of the drain, so the drain flows freely under the canal"},{"key":"b","text":"The high flood level of the drain is above the bottom of the canal trough, so the drain passes under the canal under pressure"},{"key":"c","text":"The drain bed is above the canal, so the drain is carried over the canal"},{"key":"d","text":"The canal and the drain are at nearly the same level"}],"answer":"b","explanation":"In an ordinary aqueduct the canal is carried over the drain and the high flood level (HFL) of the drain stays below the canal trough, so the drain flows with a free surface. When the HFL of the drain rises above the bottom of the trough, the drain water flows under the canal under pressure (siphonic action): a syphon aqueduct. A drain carried over the canal is a super-passage (or a canal syphon if the canal flows under pressure), and a level crossing is used when the two are at about the same level.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":7,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q7 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0705"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-008","src":"PAST-R2083-008","text":"Which crop has the highest resistance to water logging?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Wheat"},{"key":"b","text":"Maize"},{"key":"c","text":"Gram"},{"key":"d","text":"Rice (paddy)"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Rice is grown in standing water and tolerates a high water table and saturated soil. Wheat, maize and pulses such as gram suffer when their root zone is waterlogged, because their roots need air.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":8,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q8 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0701"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-019","src":"PAST-R2083-019","text":"Kennedy's silt theory was developed from observations on canals in","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Punjab (Upper Bari Doab canal system)"},{"key":"b","text":"Uttar Pradesh (Upper Ganga canal)"},{"key":"c","text":"Sindh"},{"key":"d","text":"Bengal"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"R. G. Kennedy studied about 22 stable channel reaches of the Upper Bari Doab canal system in Punjab and published his critical velocity relation V<sub>0</sub> = 0.55 m D<sup>0.64</sup> in 1895, where m is the critical velocity ratio. Lacey's regime theory came later.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":19,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q19 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0702"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-021","src":"PAST-R2083-021","text":"The frequency of irrigation (irrigation interval) is expressed in","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Days"},{"key":"b","text":"Millimetres"},{"key":"c","text":"Cubic metres per second"},{"key":"d","text":"Hectares per cumec"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"The irrigation interval is the number of days between two successive irrigations. It equals the readily available moisture in the root zone (a depth) divided by the daily consumptive use (depth per day). Depth of irrigation is in mm, discharge in m<sup>3</sup>/s and duty in hectares per cumec.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":21,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q21 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0701"}]},{"name":"Hydropower","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-015","src":"PAST-R2083-015","text":"The purpose of a tailrace in a hydropower scheme is to","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Carry water from the reservoir to the turbines"},{"key":"b","text":"Absorb water hammer pressure in the penstock"},{"key":"c","text":"Return the water leaving the turbines to the river"},{"key":"d","text":"Remove sediment before the water enters the headrace"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"The tailrace, a channel or tunnel, takes the water from the draft tube outlets of the turbines back to the river. The headrace and penstock bring water to the turbines, a surge tank absorbs water hammer, and a settling basin removes sediment.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":15,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q15 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0805"}]},{"name":"Transportation","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-011","src":"PAST-R2083-011","text":"The first priority in traffic engineering is","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Safety of road users"},{"key":"b","text":"Higher vehicle speed"},{"key":"c","text":"Lower construction cost"},{"key":"d","text":"More parking space"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Traffic engineering aims at the safe, efficient and convenient movement of people and goods. Safety comes first; speed, capacity, economy and comfort are pursued only within safe limits.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":11,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q11 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0904"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-012","src":"PAST-R2083-012","text":"In a flexible pavement, the wheel load is transmitted to the lower layers mainly by","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Flexural (slab) action of the surface course"},{"key":"b","text":"Tension in the bituminous binder"},{"key":"c","text":"Grain-to-grain transfer through the points of contact of the aggregates"},{"key":"d","text":"Direct bearing of the tyre on the subgrade"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"A flexible pavement has little flexural strength. The load passes down by grain-to-grain contact through the granular layers and spreads over a wider area with depth, so the stress reaching the subgrade is small. A rigid (concrete) pavement carries load by slab (flexural) action instead.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":12,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q12 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0905"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-014","src":"PAST-R2083-014","text":"Bitumen of grade 80/100 means that its","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Penetration is 8 to 10 mm"},{"key":"b","text":"Penetration is 80 to 100 mm"},{"key":"c","text":"Softening point is 80 to 100 °C"},{"key":"d","text":"Ductility is 80 to 100 cm"}],"answer":"a","explanation":"Penetration is measured in units of 0.1 mm: the depth a standard needle (100 g, 5 s, 25 °C) sinks into the bitumen. A penetration of 80 to 100 units is 8 to 10 mm, so 80/100 is a fairly soft grade.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":14,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q14 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0903"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-018","src":"PAST-R2083-018","text":"Which of the following is NOT shown in the elevation (longitudinal section) of a road?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Existing ground level"},{"key":"b","text":"Formation level"},{"key":"c","text":"Gradient"},{"key":"d","text":"Width of the carriageway"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"The longitudinal section (profile) of a road shows the chainages, existing ground levels, formation levels, gradients, vertical curves and depths of cut or fill. The widths of the carriageway and shoulders are horizontal dimensions, shown on the plan and cross-sections.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":18,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q18 · 1 mark"},"topic":"ACiE0901"}]},{"name":"Project Planning, Design and Implementation","questions":[{"id":"PAST-R2083-009","src":"PAST-R2083-009","text":"As per the Public Procurement Regulation, 2064, what bid validity period should be specified for a bid with a cost estimate of Rs 9 crore?","options":[{"key":"a","text":"30 days"},{"key":"b","text":"60 days"},{"key":"c","text":"90 days"},{"key":"d","text":"120 days"}],"answer":"c","explanation":"Rule 54 sets the bid validity at 90 days for a cost estimate up to Rs 10 crore (100 million) and 120 days above Rs 10 crore, so a Rs 9 crore bid needs 90 days. The bid security must stay valid at least 30 days beyond the bid validity. Do not confuse this with the bid submission period, which the Public Procurement Act (Section 14) sets at a minimum of 30 days for national and 45 days for international bidding, whatever the amount.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":9,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q9 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1004"},{"id":"PAST-R2083-013","src":"PAST-R2083-013","text":"When a property has to be sold at a depressed price, below its market value, because the owner is forced to sell urgently, the value obtained is called","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Book value"},{"key":"b","text":"Scrap value"},{"key":"c","text":"Salvage value"},{"key":"d","text":"Distress value"}],"answer":"d","explanation":"Distress value is the reduced price realised in a forced or urgent sale. Book value is the original cost less depreciation, salvage value is the value of a structure at the end of its useful life without dismantling it, and scrap value is the value of its materials after dismantling.","marks":1,"source":{"kind":"past","set":"past-r2083","question":13,"marks":1,"date":"2083 Asoj","shift":"Candidate recall","reference":"2083 Exam Recall, Q13 · 1 mark"},"topic":"AALL1002"}]}]};
